3. Dynamic Pricing Requirements
•	Prices automatically decrease as expiration approaches
The system shall automatically reduce the selling price of surplus food as its expiration time approaches (without falling below the set minimum price). The purpose is to encourage customers to buy available food before it expires and goes to waste.
•	Demand affects the speed of price reductions.
•	High demand can slow price reductions.
•	Low demand can accelerate price reductions.
The system shall consider customer demand when the speed at which the price is reduced. At high demand the system will slow down price reductions to preserve profits, whereas when the available food is lacking in demand the system will accelerate price reductions to encourage purchases.

•	Vendors can set a minimum acceptable price
The system shall allow vendors to set a specific minimum price for each food listing. The price engine shall never reduce the price below this minimum, regardless of time remaining or demand. Once the minimum price is reached, further price reductions halt. 
•	Linear, exponential, and stepped pricing curves are supported.
The system shall support three pricing approaches:
-	Linear pricing: the price decreases at a steady rate over time.
-	Exponential pricing: the price decreases slowly at first and then rapidly decreases as time passes.
-	Stepped pricing: the price decreases by predefined amounts or percentages at specific time intervals.
•	Prices are intended to update approximately every 15–30 minutes.
The system shall recalculate prices approximately every 15-30 minutes while a listing remains available. When a new price is calculated, the system shall update the price displayed to customers unless its below the minimum. The system shall stop updating prices when the listing becomes unavailable or reaches the minimum. 
Briefly explain the proposed pricing formula:
Price(t) = BasePrice × DecayFunction(timeRemaining) × DemandMultiplier
The “DecayFunction(timeRemaining)” is responsible for determining how the price changes over time, while the “DemandMultiplier” adjusts the effect of these reductions. Higher demand will slow down price reductions, whereas low demand will speed them up. 
The calculated selling price shall never fall below the vendors set minimum selling price. The exact decay function, demand threshold, and multipliers shall be defined during system design and testing.
Focus on defining how the system should work. You do not need to program the pricing algorithm.
